From fd508976ce0a3dace81dbd9f7542e14b721bdcf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 11:30:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] x11, cairo: window shadows and rounded corners for windows
 the gui decorates

When the gui draws a window's decorations (GSBackHandlesWindowDecorations
or GSX11HandlesWindowDecorations NO), as a theme drawing GNOME's header
bar does, the window has neither the window manager's frame nor its
shadow.  GTK's client-side decorated windows draw their own shadow in a
transparent margin round the window, tell the window manager where the
window is within the margin (_GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS), and round their top
corners.  This does the same for GNUstep windows, when a theme asks for
it with GSBackWindowShadows (and GSBackWindowCornerRadius) in its
GSThemeDomain.  Nothing changes unless the default is set.

A styled, buffered window gets the margin only when the gui draws its
decorations, the window manager lists _GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS in
_NET_SUPPORTED (otherwise it would place, snap and tile the window by
the margin's edge) and a compositing manager runs (otherwise the margin
would show black).  The X window is then the frame plus the margin, with
the 32-bit visual from the previous change; -_offsets::::for: replaces
-styleoffsets::::: wherever frames, rectangles and points are converted,
so the gui keeps working in its frame's coordinates.  The input shape
lets presses in the margin through to what is below, except a 12 point
band round a resizable window, where the gui resizes from, as GTK's
windows do.  The margin is dropped while the window manager has the
window maximized, fullscreen or tiled, and while no compositing manager
runs (XFixes tells when one starts or stops).  Margin, radius and band
are in points and grow with GSScaleFactor.

The minimum and maximum size hints are for the X window, so they include
the margin and follow it, as GTK's do.  The window manager fits the X
window to a new margin keeping the visible part's size, so a margin that
grows is set before the larger hints, and one that shrinks after the
smaller ones: otherwise the window manager holds the window, in between,
at a minimum meant for the other margin.  (With the hints first, KWin 6.3
restored a maximized window to its minimum size plus the margin, 20
pixels larger each way than before.)

When the margin changes on a maximize or restore, the window manager
usually configures the window for the old margin first, so the gui would
see three resizes for one maximize; the frame is held back until the
ConfigureNotify events stop (50 ms with none, 300 ms at most) and sent
once.

XGCairoModernSurface draws the shadow: black, 19% at the sides of the
window fading over about 30 pixels, weaker above and stronger below, as
measured from libadwaita 1.7 windows under Mutter (only the numbers are
taken; no code).  It is computed once as a template of corners and
edges and put together in the X server for each window size.  Exposes
copy the contents and the shadow as rectangles, and only the four
corners go through a rounded clip.  An OpenGL view's X window is placed
with the new offsets too.

Testing: two tests, each forking a stand-in window manager that knows
_GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS (Tests/x11/FakeCSDWindowManager.h, which grants every
configure request and never sets a state itself) and the stand-in
compositing manager from the previous change.  Both need a display with
neither (a private Xvfb) and skip otherwise, and set their defaults in
the argument domain, for the test process only.

- Tests/x11/shadowmargin.m works with the display server alone and
  reads back what the window manager is told: _GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS, the
  depth and size of the X window and its input shape, as exact values.
  It plays a maximize and a restore by setting _NET_WM_STATE (and, with
  a minimum size set, checks that the size hints change before
  _GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS on the maximize and after it on the restore), stops
  and restarts the compositing manager, sets a program resize, and
  repeats with GSScaleFactor 2.  Negative cases guard against the margin
  appearing where it must not: without the default, for a non-retained
  or borderless window, for a window made with no compositing manager,
  and under a window manager without _GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS.  Without this
  change the 14 positive checks fail and the 5 negative ones pass; with
  it all 19 pass.  With the hints set before the margin, as first
  written, the restore's order check fails and the other 18 pass.
- Tests/x11/shadowdrawing.m makes a real NSWindow and reads the alpha of
  its X window's pixels back: the contents opaque, the top corners
  rounded off, about 19% black beside the window fading to nearly
  nothing at the margin's edge, and stronger below than above.  The
  bounds are loose enough for rounding (values seen: 46, 19 and 3 across
  the margin, 51 below, 22 above) but would catch a missing shadow,
  unrounded corners or a shadow drawn over the window.  Without this
  change its first check fails ("a 32-bit X window larger than its
  frame") and the rest don't run; with it all 8 pass.  It was run
  against libs-gui master; with a libs-gui older than 8a092cfa7
  (libs-gui#816), the test process aborts before its first check
  (-[GSCInlineString copy] sent to a deallocated instance: the font
  roles kept the backend's default font names unretained).

Not covered by these tests: the frame being held back while the margin
settles, the _GTK_EDGE_CONSTRAINTS (tiled) case, and the OpenGL view's
placement.

Outside libs-back, the Adwaita theme's own check under GNOME Shell 48.7
(X11, on a private Xvfb; make check-mutter-shadow in
plugins-themes-Adwaita) was run against libs-back master with this
change and the previous one, and libs-gui master with its theme-defaults
patch.  18 of its 19 checks pass, among them: one resize reaches the gui
on a maximize and two on the restore, the margin is gone while maximized
or tiled and back after, and a press 12 pixels outside the window
resizes it while one 13 pixels out reaches the window behind.  The 19th
is a menu's shadow, which the next change adds.  With the rest of the
series this belongs to, the same checks passed under KWin 6.3.6, Xfwm4
4.20.0 and Openbox 3.6.1 with picom 12.5 (make check-wms).

AI assistance: this change, its tests and this description were written
with Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
